IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- Work Health and Safety Act 2020 (Western Australia) -- who owed me a duty at work, and what it required (ss 5, 7, 8,
-- 17, 19, 20, 21, 27, 28, 29, with regs 35, 36 and 40); what the duty holder who failed me, or I, can be made to pay
-- (ss 30, 30A, 31-33); whether what happened to me is a notifiable incident and what then must be done (ss 35-39, with
-- reg 699); whether I must be consulted (s 47); my right to stop unsafe work and what follows (ss 84-88); my protection
-- from discrimination and coercion for raising a safety issue (ss 104-106, 108, 110, 112, 113, 115); and three general
-- rules that reach me (ss 267, 272, 273).
--
-- SEAT: the worker -- here the night security officer employed by a security contractor and posted to an office tower,
-- who falls on a wet stairwell on patrol. Every decision is phrased as the worker would ask it: "Am I a worker?",
-- "Does this person owe me the primary duty of care?", "Is this person a person with management or control of my
-- workplace?", "Is my injury a notifiable incident?", "May I cease or refuse to carry out this work?", "Was what was
-- done to me discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason?". Where the Act addresses only the duty holder, the
-- regulator, an inspector, a court or the Tribunal, it is encoded as what that means for the worker: who must notify,
-- by when, what may be done to the site, what the person who failed me can be fined, what I may ask the Tribunal for.
--
-- Provisions covered: s 5(1), (4), (5), (7), (10) ("volunteer association"); s 4A(1) ("officer"); s 7(1), (3); s 8(1);
-- s 17; s 19(1), (2), (4); s 20; s 21; s 27(1); s 28; s 29; s 30 ("serious harm"); s 30A(1), (3); s 31(1)-(3);
-- s 32(1); s 33; s 35; s 36(1); s 37; s 38(1), (4)(b), (7); s 39(1), (3); s 47(1); s 84; s 85(1)-(3), (6), (7); s 86;
-- s 88; s 104; s 105; s 106; s 108(1); s 110(2); s 112(1)-(4); s 113(1)-(3); s 115; s 267; s 272; s 273.
-- Work Health and Safety (General) Regulations 2022 regs 35, 36, 40 and 699.
--
-- Edition: compilation from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc 49498, Act No. 36 of 2020, retrieved 7 September 2026), as
-- deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49498.txt. The deposit names no compilation or reprint number; its
-- compilation table ends with the Industrial Relations Legislation Amendment Act 2024 s 160 (No. 43 of 2024, in
-- operation 31 January 2025) and lists the Petroleum Legislation Amendment Act 2024 Pt 5 Div 15 (in operation 28 May
-- 2026); ss 428-430 and 431(2) of that Act are uncommenced. Regulations: Work Health and Safety (General) Regulations
-- 2022, compilation version 01-c0-00 current from 1 July 2026 (mrdoc 49725, retrieved 19 October 2026), as deposited at
-- ../../../work-health-and-safety-general-regulations-2022/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49725.txt; its compilation
-- table ends with the Industrial Relations Regulations Amendment (Fees and Charges) Regulations 2026 Pt 6 (SL 2026/94,
-- in operation 1 July 2026).
--
-- NUMBERS. Every fine in the Act and in the regulations used here is stated in dollars in the text; none is indexed
-- or set elsewhere. The regulations prescribe additional serious illnesses for s 36 (reg 699). A search of the
-- deposited regulations found no regulation prescribing a further dangerous incident for s 37, nor any injury,
-- illness or incident of an excluded "prescribed kind".
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the duties of designers, manufacturers, importers, suppliers, installers and WHS service providers
-- (ss 22-26A); the self-employed person's duty (s 19(5)); volunteers and unincorporated associations (s 34); the
-- authorisations of Part 4; consultation with other duty holders (s 46) and the matters on which consultation is
-- required (s 49, one input); health and safety representatives' election and powers other than the s 85 direction
-- (Part 5 Divisions 3-4, 7); health and safety committees; issue resolution (Part 5 Division 5); suitable
-- alternative work as a direction (s 87); requests for an inspector (ss 89, 89A); the criminal-proceedings proof
-- rules other than the s 110(2) presumption and the s 111 orders; misrepresentation (s 109); the regulator's and
-- inspectors' powers and notices (Parts 8-10); review of decisions (Part 12); legal proceedings (Part 13) other than
-- what s 267 says of civil liability; the insurance-against-fines rule (s 272A); codes of practice; and the
-- regulations' hazard-specific Parts (falls from one level to another under reg 78 are not a slip on a stair).
-- Whether a risk was eliminated or minimised "so far as is reasonably practicable" (s 18) is a judgment that would be
-- a court's: it is always an input.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill, from the seat of the
-- individual to whom the law is applied, with the design defaults ruled on 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Work Health and Safety Act 2020 (WA) -- the worker`

§§ `ss 5, 7, 8 -- worker, person conducting a business or undertaking, workplace`

-- s 7(1): "A person is a worker if the person carries out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or
-- undertaking, including work as -- (a) an employee; or (b) a contractor or subcontractor; or (c) an employee of a
-- contractor or subcontractor; or (d) an employee of a labour hire company ...; or (e) an outworker; or (f) an
-- apprentice or trainee; or (g) a student gaining work experience; or (h) a volunteer; or (i) a person of a prescribed
-- class." The list is inclusive: any capacity will do. s 7(3): the person conducting the business or undertaking "is
-- also a worker if the person is an individual who carries out work in that business or undertaking".
-- (Gloss: a "worker" is anyone who does work for a business, whatever the contract.)
@export Am I a worker under the Work Health and Safety Act 2020 (WA) (s 7)?
GIVEN `I carry out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you do work, as an employee, contractor, employee of a contractor, labour hire worker, outworker, apprentice, trainee, work experience student, volunteer or otherwise, for a business or undertaking
      `I am an individual conducting the business and I carry out work in it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you run the business yourself and also work in it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a worker` IF
        `I carry out work in any capacity for a person conducting a business or undertaking`
    OR  `I am an individual conducting the business and I carry out work in it`

-- s 5(1): a person conducts a business or undertaking "(a) whether the person conducts the business or undertaking
-- alone or with others; and (b) whether or not the business or undertaking is conducted for profit or gain".
-- s 5(4): "An individual does not conduct a business or undertaking to the extent that the individual is engaged solely
-- as a worker in, or as an officer of, that business or undertaking." s 5(5): "A local government member does not
-- conduct a business or undertaking." s 5(7): "A volunteer association does not conduct a business or undertaking";
-- s 5(10): a "volunteer association means a group of volunteers working together for 1 or more community purposes
-- where none of the volunteers ... employs any person to carry out work for the volunteer association."
-- "Business or undertaking" itself is not defined: it is asked. (Gloss: a "PCBU" is whoever runs the business --
-- a company, a partnership, a sole trader, a government agency -- not the people who only work in it.)
DECLARE `The other person` HAS
    `carries on a business or undertaking, alone or with others, for profit or not` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5(1)
    `is an individual engaged in it solely as a worker or officer`                  IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5(4)
    `is a local government member`                                                   IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5(5)
    `is a group of volunteers working together for community purposes`               IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5(10)
    `any of the volunteers employs a person to carry out work for the group`         IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 5(10)

GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a volunteer association` MEANS
        `the person`'s `is a group of volunteers working together for community purposes`
    AND (NOT `the person`'s `any of the volunteers employs a person to carry out work for the group`)

@export Does this person conduct a business or undertaking (s 5)?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person` @desc The person whose duty you are asking about: your employer, the building's manager, a tenant, a contractor
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this person conduct a business or undertaking` IF
        `the person`'s `carries on a business or undertaking, alone or with others, for profit or not`
    AND (NOT `the person`'s `is an individual engaged in it solely as a worker or officer`)
    AND (NOT `the person`'s `is a local government member`)
    AND (NOT `is a volunteer association` `the person`)

-- s 8(1): "A workplace is a place where work is carried out for a business or undertaking and includes any place where
-- a worker goes, or is likely to be, while at work." s 8(2): "place" includes a vehicle, vessel, aircraft, waters and
-- installations. (Gloss: wherever work is done, or a worker goes while working -- a stairwell on a patrol route included.)
@export Is this place a workplace (s 8)?
GIVEN `work is carried out there for a business or undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether work is done at the place for a business
      `a worker goes there, or is likely to be there, while at work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or another worker go to the place, or are likely to be there, while working
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this place a workplace` IF
        `work is carried out there for a business or undertaking`
    OR  `a worker goes there, or is likely to be there, while at work`

§§ `ss 19-21 -- who owed me a duty of care`

-- s 19(1): "A person conducting a business or undertaking must ensure, so far as is reasonably practicable, the health
-- and safety of -- (a) workers engaged, or caused to be engaged, by the person; and (b) workers whose activities in
-- carrying out work are influenced or directed by the person, while the workers are at work in the business or
-- undertaking." (Gloss: the "primary duty of care" -- owed by the business that engaged me, or that directs my work.)
@export Does this person owe me the primary duty of care (s 19(1))?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person` @desc The person whose duty you are asking about
      `I am a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker (s 7)
      `the person engaged me, or caused me to be engaged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person employed or contracted you, or caused you to be engaged (for example by contracting your employer to supply you)
      `the person influences or directs my activities in carrying out work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person directs or influences how you do your work
      `I was at work in its business or undertaking` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were at work in that person's business or undertaking at the time
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this person owe me the primary duty of care` IF
        `Does this person conduct a business or undertaking` `the person`
    AND `I am a worker`
    AND (`the person engaged me, or caused me to be engaged` OR `the person influences or directs my activities in carrying out work`)
    AND `I was at work in its business or undertaking`

-- s 19(2): a person conducting a business or undertaking "must ensure, so far as is reasonably practicable, that the
-- health and safety of other persons is not put at risk from work carried out as part of the conduct of the business
-- or undertaking". (Gloss: a business owes everyone else a duty not to endanger them by its own work -- a tenant's
-- cleaners mopping a stair the officer must use, for example.)
@export Must this person ensure its work does not put my health and safety at risk (s 19(2))?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person` @desc The person whose duty you are asking about
      `its work could put my health or safety at risk` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether work done as part of that person's business could put you at risk
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must this person ensure its work does not put me at risk` IF
        `Does this person conduct a business or undertaking` `the person`
    AND `its work could put my health or safety at risk`

-- s 19(4): if "(a) a worker occupies accommodation that is owned by or under the management or control of the person
-- conducting the business or undertaking; and (b) the occupancy is necessary for the purposes of the worker's engagement
-- because other accommodation is not reasonably available", the person must, so far as is reasonably practicable,
-- maintain the premises so that the worker is not exposed to risks to health and safety.
@export Must my employer maintain the accommodation I live in for work (s 19(4))?
GIVEN `the accommodation is owned, managed or controlled by the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business owns, manages or controls the place you live
      `I must live there for my engagement because other accommodation is not reasonably available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether living there is necessary for your work because nothing else is reasonably available
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must my employer maintain my accommodation` IF
        `the accommodation is owned, managed or controlled by the business`
    AND `I must live there for my engagement because other accommodation is not reasonably available`

-- s 20(1): "person with management or control of a workplace means a person conducting a business or undertaking to the
-- extent that the business or undertaking involves the management or control, in whole or in part, of the workplace but
-- does not include -- (a) the occupier of a residence, unless the residence is occupied for the purposes of, or as part
-- of, the conduct of a business or undertaking; or (b) a prescribed person." s 20(2): that person "must ensure, so far
-- as is reasonably practicable, that the workplace, the means of entering and exiting the workplace and anything arising
-- from the workplace are without risks to the health and safety of any person."
-- s 21 is the same shape for "fixtures, fittings or plant at a workplace".
-- Whether a tenant controls a common stairwell is a fact about its lease and its conduct: asked, not guessed.
-- (Gloss: the building manager, owner or tenant who controls the place -- or part of it -- must keep it, and the ways
-- in and out, safe for anyone, whoever employs them.)
DECLARE `Control of the place` HAS
    `its business involves management or control of the place, in whole or in part` IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 20(1), s 21(1)
    `the place is a residence it occupies`                                            IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 20(1)(a)
    `the residence is occupied for the purposes of a business or undertaking`         IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 20(1)(a)
    `it is a prescribed person`                                                       IS A BOOLEAN  -- s 20(1)(b)

GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person`
      `control` IS A `Control of the place`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`has management or control within section 20(1)` MEANS
        `Does this person conduct a business or undertaking` `the person`
    AND `control`'s `its business involves management or control of the place, in whole or in part`
    AND ((NOT `control`'s `the place is a residence it occupies`) OR `control`'s `the residence is occupied for the purposes of a business or undertaking`)
    AND (NOT `control`'s `it is a prescribed person`)

@export Must this person, as a person with management or control of my workplace, keep it and its ways in and out without risk to me (s 20)?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person` @desc The person whose duty you are asking about
      `control` IS A `Control of the place` @desc What part, if any, the person has in managing or controlling the place
      `the place is a workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the place is a workplace (s 8)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this person owe me the workplace duty` IF
        `has management or control within section 20(1)` `the person` `control`
    AND `the place is a workplace`

@export Must this person keep the fixtures, fittings or plant it manages or controls at my workplace without risk to me (s 21)?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person` @desc The person whose duty you are asking about
      `control` IS A `Control of the place` @desc Read here as control of the fixtures, fittings or plant (the handrail, the lighting, the lift) rather than of the place
      `the place is a workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they are at a workplace (s 8)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this person owe me the fixtures duty` IF
        `has management or control within section 20(1)` `the person` `control`
    AND `the place is a workplace`

§§ `s 17, regs 35, 36, 40 -- what the duty requires`

-- s 17: "A duty imposed on a person to ensure health and safety requires the person -- (a) to eliminate risks to health
-- and safety, so far as is reasonably practicable; and (b) if it is not reasonably practicable to eliminate risks to
-- health and safety, to minimise those risks so far as is reasonably practicable." Reg 35 repeats it.
-- Reg 36(3): if elimination is not reasonably practicable, "minimise risks, so far as is reasonably practicable, by
-- doing 1 or more of the following -- (a) substituting ...; (b) isolating the hazard ...; (c) implementing engineering
-- controls." (4): "If a risk then remains ... administrative controls." (5): "If a risk then remains ... suitable
-- personal protective equipment."
-- Reg 40(a), (c): the person conducting a business at a workplace must ensure, so far as reasonably practicable, that
-- the workplace allows persons "to enter and exit and to move about without risk", and that "floors and other surfaces
-- are designed, installed and maintained to allow work to be carried out without risk".
-- (Gloss: the "hierarchy of control" -- remove the hazard if you reasonably can; if not, substitute, isolate or
-- engineer it out; then rules and procedures; then protective equipment, last.)
DECLARE `Control step` IS ONE OF
    `eliminate the risk`
    `substitute, isolate or engineer`
    `add administrative controls`
    `provide personal protective equipment`
    `nothing further is reasonably practicable`

@export What must the person who owes me the duty do next about this risk (s 17; regs 35, 36)?
GIVEN `eliminating the risk is reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk could reasonably be removed altogether (for a wet stair: fixing the leak, or closing the stair)
      `a substitution, isolation or engineering control is reasonably practicable and not in place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether such a control (a non-slip surface, a drain, a barrier) could reasonably be added and is not there
      `an administrative control is reasonably practicable and not in place` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a rule or procedure (warning signs, a cleaning roster, a changed patrol route) could reasonably be added and is not there
      `personal protective equipment is reasonably practicable and not provided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether suitable protective equipment (slip-resistant footwear) could reasonably be provided and is not
GIVETH A `Control step`
DECIDE `What must be done next about this risk` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `eliminating the risk is reasonably practicable` THEN `eliminate the risk`
      IF `a substitution, isolation or engineering control is reasonably practicable and not in place` THEN `substitute, isolate or engineer`
      IF `an administrative control is reasonably practicable and not in place` THEN `add administrative controls`
      IF `personal protective equipment is reasonably practicable and not provided` THEN `provide personal protective equipment`
      OTHERWISE `nothing further is reasonably practicable`

§§ `ss 4A, 27, 28, 29 -- officers, me, and others at the workplace`

-- s 4A(1): "officer (a) means -- (i) an officer within the meaning of the Corporations Act 2001 (Commonwealth) section 9
-- other than a partner in a partnership; or (ii) an officer of the Crown ...; or (iii) an officer of a public
-- corporation ...; but (b) does not include -- (i) the Governor ...; (ii) a Minister ...; or (iii) a local government
-- member." s 27(1): "If a person conducting a business or undertaking has a duty or obligation under this Act, an
-- officer of the person conducting the business or undertaking must exercise due diligence to ensure that the person
-- conducting the business or undertaking complies with that duty or obligation."
-- (Gloss: the directors and senior managers of the business that owes me a duty must themselves take reasonable steps
-- to see that it is met.)
DECLARE `Officer status` IS ONE OF
    `a Corporations Act section 9 officer who is not a partner`
    `an officer of the Crown under section 4A(2)`
    `an officer of a public corporation under section 4A(3)`
    `the Governor, a Minister or a local government member acting as such`
    `not an officer`

GIVEN `status` IS AN `Officer status`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is an officer` MEANS
    CONSIDER `status`
    WHEN `a Corporations Act section 9 officer who is not a partner` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an officer of the Crown under section 4A(2)` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an officer of a public corporation under section 4A(3)` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

@export Must this officer exercise due diligence to see that the business meets its duty to me (s 4A, s 27(1))?
GIVEN `status` IS AN `Officer status` @desc What position the person holds in the business that owes you a duty
      `the business owes me a duty under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business the person is an officer of owes you a duty or obligation under the Act
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must this officer exercise due diligence for me` IF
        `is an officer` `status`
    AND `the business owes me a duty under the Act`

-- s 28: "While at work, a worker must -- (a) take reasonable care for the worker's own health and safety; and (b) take
-- reasonable care that the worker's acts or omissions do not adversely affect the health and safety of other persons;
-- and (c) comply, so far as the worker is reasonably able, with any reasonable instruction that is given by the person
-- conducting the business or undertaking to allow the person to comply with this Act; and (d) cooperate with any
-- reasonable policy or procedure ... that has been notified to workers."
@export Must I comply with this instruction from the business (s 28(c))?
GIVEN `I am at work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are at work
      `the business gave the instruction to allow it to comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business gave the instruction so that it can meet its duties under the Act
      `the instruction is reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the instruction is a reasonable one
      `I am reasonably able to comply` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are reasonably able to comply
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I comply with this instruction` IF
        `I am at work`
    AND `the business gave the instruction to allow it to comply with the Act`
    AND `the instruction is reasonable`
    AND `I am reasonably able to comply`

-- s 29: "A person at a workplace (whether or not the person has another duty under this Part) must -- (a) take
-- reasonable care for the person's own health and safety; and (b) take reasonable care that the person's acts or
-- omissions do not adversely affect the health and safety of other persons; and (c) comply ... with any reasonable
-- instruction" of the person conducting the business. (Gloss: a visitor, a tenant's staff member, anyone at the
-- workplace, owes me reasonable care.)
@export Must this other person at my workplace take reasonable care not to affect my health and safety (s 29)?
GIVEN `the person is at a workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is at the workplace
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must this person at the workplace take reasonable care for me` IF `the person is at a workplace`

§§ `ss 30-33 -- what the person who failed me, or I, can be made to pay`

-- s 30: "serious harm, in relation to an individual, means an illness or injury that -- (a) endangers, or is likely to
-- endanger, the individual's life; or (b) results, or is likely to result, in permanent injury or harm to the
-- individual's health." s 31(3) is in the same words for Category 1.
@export Is my injury serious harm (s 30, s 31(3))?
GIVEN `it endangered, or was likely to endanger, my life` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the injury or illness put your life in danger, or was likely to
      `it results, or is likely to result, in permanent injury or harm to my health` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it leaves, or is likely to leave, a permanent injury or harm to your health
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my injury serious harm` IF
        `it endangered, or was likely to endanger, my life`
    OR  `it results, or is likely to result, in permanent injury or harm to my health`

-- s 30A(1): a person with a health and safety duty as a PCBU commits industrial manslaughter if its conduct causes a
-- death, is a failure to comply with its duty, and is engaged in "(i) knowing that the conduct is likely to cause the
-- death of, or serious harm to, an individual; and (ii) in disregard of that likelihood". Penalty "(a) for an
-- individual, imprisonment for 20 years and a fine of $5 000 000; (b) for a body corporate, a fine of $10 000 000".
-- s 30A(3): an officer whose neglect, consent or connivance the PCBU's conduct is attributable to, with the same
-- knowledge and disregard: "imprisonment for 20 years and a fine of $5 000 000".
-- s 31(1) (Category 1, duty as a PCBU, failure causing death or serious harm): "(a) for an individual, imprisonment for
-- 5 years and a fine of $680 000; (b) for a body corporate, a fine of $3 500 000". s 31(2) (duty otherwise than as a
-- PCBU): officer, 5 years and $680 000; other individual, 5 years and $340 000; body corporate $3 500 000.
-- s 32(1) (Category 2, failure exposing an individual to a risk of death, injury or harm): individual as PCBU or officer
-- $350 000; other individual $170 000; body corporate $1 800 000.
-- s 33 (Category 3, failure to comply): individual as PCBU or officer $120 000; other individual $55 000; body
-- corporate $570 000.
-- Which offence is proved, and whether the knowledge and disregard were present, is for the court: the knowledge is an
-- input, and the answer is the most that can be imposed for the gravest offence the facts fit.
DECLARE `Duty holder` IS ONE OF
    `an individual conducting the business or undertaking`
    `an individual who is an officer`
    `another individual, such as a worker`
    `a body corporate`

DECLARE `What the failure did` IS ONE OF
    `caused a death`
    `caused serious harm`
    `exposed someone to a risk of death, injury or harm`
    `no more than a failure to comply`

GIVEN `who` IS A `Duty holder`
      `outcome` IS A `What the failure did`
      `knowing death or serious harm was likely, and in disregard of it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is industrial manslaughter` MEANS
        `outcome` EQUALS `caused a death`
    AND `knowing death or serious harm was likely, and in disregard of it`
    AND (NOT (`who` EQUALS `another individual, such as a worker`))

-- The three scales of fines for an individual as PCBU or officer, another individual, and a body corporate.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Duty holder`
      `as PCBU or officer` IS A NUMBER
      `other individual` IS A NUMBER
      `body corporate` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the fine on the scale` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who`
    WHEN `an individual conducting the business or undertaking` THEN `as PCBU or officer`
    WHEN `an individual who is an officer` THEN `as PCBU or officer`
    WHEN `another individual, such as a worker` THEN `other individual`
    WHEN `a body corporate` THEN `body corporate`

@export What is the most this duty holder can be fined for failing in its health and safety duty (ss 30A-33)?
GIVEN `who` IS A `Duty holder` @desc Who failed in the duty: an individual running the business, an officer, another individual such as a worker (including you), or a company
      `outcome` IS A `What the failure did` @desc What the failure did: caused a death, caused serious harm, exposed someone to a risk, or none of these
      `knowing death or serious harm was likely, and in disregard of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on the court's finding, the conduct was engaged in knowing it was likely to cause death or serious harm and in disregard of that (only matters for a death)
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most the duty holder can be fined` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `is industrial manslaughter` `who` `outcome` `knowing death or serious harm was likely, and in disregard of it`
        THEN `the fine on the scale` `who` 5000000 5000000 10000000
      IF `outcome` EQUALS `caused a death` OR `outcome` EQUALS `caused serious harm`
        THEN `the fine on the scale` `who` 680000 340000 3500000
      IF `outcome` EQUALS `exposed someone to a risk of death, injury or harm`
        THEN `the fine on the scale` `who` 350000 170000 1800000
      OTHERWISE `the fine on the scale` `who` 120000 55000 570000

@export What is the longest term of imprisonment, in years, this duty holder faces for failing in its duty (ss 30A, 31)?
GIVEN `who` IS A `Duty holder` @desc Who failed in the duty
      `outcome` IS A `What the failure did` @desc What the failure did
      `knowing death or serious harm was likely, and in disregard of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, on the court's finding, the conduct was knowing and in disregard of the likelihood of death or serious harm
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest prison term in years` IS
    BRANCH
      IF `who` EQUALS `a body corporate` THEN 0
      IF `is industrial manslaughter` `who` `outcome` `knowing death or serious harm was likely, and in disregard of it` THEN 20
      IF `outcome` EQUALS `caused a death` OR `outcome` EQUALS `caused serious harm` THEN 5
      OTHERWISE 0

§§ `ss 35-39, reg 699 -- notifiable incidents`

-- s 36(1): "serious injury or illness, of a person, means an injury or illness -- (a) that requires the person to have
-- immediate treatment as an inpatient in a hospital; or (b) that requires the person to have immediate treatment for --
-- (i) the amputation of any part of the person's body; or (ii) a serious head injury; or (iii) a serious eye injury; or
-- (iv) a serious burn; or (v) the separation of the person's skin from an underlying tissue ...; or (vi) a spinal
-- injury; or (vii) the loss of a bodily function; or (viii) serious lacerations; or (c) that requires the person to
-- have treatment by a medical practitioner within 48 hours of exposure to a substance; or (d) that occurs in a remote
-- location and requires the person to be transferred urgently to a medical facility for treatment; or (e) that, in the
-- opinion of a medical practitioner, is likely to prevent the person from being able to do the person's normal work for
-- at least 10 days after the day on which the injury or illness occurs, and includes any other injury or illness
-- prescribed by the regulations".
-- Reg 699: "For the purposes of section 36 of the Act, each of the following conditions is a serious illness -- (a) any
-- infection to which the carrying out of work is a significant contributing factor ...; (b) the following occupational
-- zoonoses contracted in the course of work involving handling or contact with animals ... -- (i) Q fever; (ii) Anthrax;
-- (iii) Leptospirosis; (iv) Brucellosis; (v) Hendra Virus; (vi) Avian Influenza; (vii) Psittacosis."
DECLARE `Immediate treatment` IS ONE OF
    `amputation of a part of the body`
    `a serious head injury`
    `a serious eye injury`
    `a serious burn`
    `separation of skin from underlying tissue`
    `a spinal injury`
    `loss of a bodily function`
    `serious lacerations`
    `none of the listed treatments`

DECLARE `Prescribed illness` IS ONE OF
    `an infection to which carrying out work was a significant contributing factor`
    `Q fever`
    `Anthrax`
    `Leptospirosis`
    `Brucellosis`
    `Hendra Virus`
    `Avian Influenza`
    `Psittacosis`
    `none of the regulation 699 conditions`

DECLARE `My injury or illness` HAS
    `needs immediate treatment as a hospital inpatient`                                    IS A BOOLEAN               -- s 36(1)(a)
    `needs immediate treatment for`                                                        IS AN `Immediate treatment` -- s 36(1)(b)
    `needs a medical practitioner's treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance`   IS A BOOLEAN               -- s 36(1)(c)
    `occurred in a remote location and needs urgent transfer to a medical facility`         IS A BOOLEAN               -- s 36(1)(d)
    `a medical practitioner thinks it likely to keep me from my normal work for 10 days or more` IS A BOOLEAN          -- s 36(1)(e)
    `is a regulation 699 condition`                                                        IS A `Prescribed illness`   -- reg 699
    `was contracted in work handling or in contact with animals or animal products`         IS A BOOLEAN               -- reg 699(b)

GIVEN `illness` IS A `Prescribed illness`
      `animal work` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a prescribed serious illness` MEANS
    CONSIDER `illness`
    WHEN `none of the regulation 699 conditions` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `an infection to which carrying out work was a significant contributing factor` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE `animal work`

@export Is my injury or illness a serious injury or illness (s 36; reg 699)?
GIVEN `mine` IS A `My injury or illness` @desc What your injury or illness required, and what a doctor thinks of it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my injury or illness serious` IF
        `mine`'s `needs immediate treatment as a hospital inpatient`
    OR  (NOT (`mine`'s `needs immediate treatment for` EQUALS `none of the listed treatments`))
    OR  `mine`'s `needs a medical practitioner's treatment within 48 hours of exposure to a substance`
    OR  `mine`'s `occurred in a remote location and needs urgent transfer to a medical facility`
    OR  `mine`'s `a medical practitioner thinks it likely to keep me from my normal work for 10 days or more`
    OR  `is a prescribed serious illness` (`mine`'s `is a regulation 699 condition`) (`mine`'s `was contracted in work handling or in contact with animals or animal products`)

-- s 37: "dangerous incident means an incident in relation to a workplace that exposes a worker or any other person to a
-- serious risk to a person's health or safety emanating from an immediate or imminent exposure to -- (a) an uncontrolled
-- escape, spillage or leakage of a substance; (b) an uncontrolled implosion, explosion or fire; (c) an uncontrolled
-- escape of gas or steam; (d) an uncontrolled escape of a pressurised substance; (e) electric shock; (f) the fall or
-- release from a height of any plant, substance or thing; (g) the collapse, overturning, failure or malfunction of, or
-- damage to, any plant that is required to be authorised ...; (h) the collapse or partial collapse of a structure; (i)
-- the collapse or failure of an excavation or of any shoring ...; (j) the inrush of water, mud or gas in workings ...;
-- (k) the interruption of the main system of ventilation in an underground excavation or tunnel; or (l) any other event
-- prescribed by the regulations". No regulation in the deposit prescribes an event under (l).
-- A person slipping on a wet stair is not on the list: the fall in (f) is of "plant, substance or thing".
DECLARE `Exposure` IS ONE OF
    `an uncontrolled escape, spillage or leakage of a substance`
    `an uncontrolled implosion, explosion or fire`
    `an uncontrolled escape of gas or steam`
    `an uncontrolled escape of a pressurised substance`
    `electric shock`
    `the fall or release from a height of plant, a substance or a thing`
    `the collapse, failure or malfunction of plant that must be authorised`
    `the collapse or partial collapse of a structure`
    `the collapse or failure of an excavation or its shoring`
    `an inrush of water, mud or gas underground`
    `interruption of the main ventilation underground`
    `none of the listed exposures`

@export Was this a dangerous incident (s 37)?
GIVEN `it exposed a person to a serious risk to health or safety at a workplace` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident exposed you or anyone to a serious risk to health or safety, in relation to a workplace
      `the exposure was immediate or imminent, to` IS AN `Exposure` @desc What the immediate or imminent exposure was to
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was this a dangerous incident` IF
        `it exposed a person to a serious risk to health or safety at a workplace`
    AND (NOT (`the exposure was immediate or imminent, to` EQUALS `none of the listed exposures`))

-- s 35: "notifiable incident means -- (a) the death of a person; or (b) a serious injury or illness of a person; or (c) a
-- dangerous incident."
@export Is what happened a notifiable incident (s 35)?
GIVEN `a person died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a person died
      `a person suffered a serious injury or illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a serious injury or illness (s 36)
      `it was a dangerous incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was a dangerous incident (s 37)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it a notifiable incident` IF
        `a person died`
    OR  `a person suffered a serious injury or illness`
    OR  `it was a dangerous incident`

-- s 38(1): "A person who conducts a business or undertaking must ensure that the regulator is notified immediately after
-- becoming aware that a notifiable incident arising out of the conduct of the business or undertaking has occurred."
-- Penalty "(a) for an individual, a fine of $12 500; (b) for a body corporate, a fine of $55 000." s 38(4)(b): a person
-- who notifies by telephone must, if required, give a written notice "within 48 hours of that requirement being made".
-- s 38(7): a record of each notifiable incident must be kept "for at least 5 years from the day that notice of the
-- incident is given".
@export Must this business notify the regulator, immediately, of what happened to me (s 38(1))?
GIVEN `the person` IS A `The other person` @desc The business whose duty you are asking about (your employer, the building's manager, a tenant)
      `it is a notifiable incident` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what happened is a notifiable incident (s 35)
      `it arose out of the conduct of that business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the incident arose out of the conduct of that person's business or undertaking
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must this business notify the regulator` IF
        `Does this person conduct a business or undertaking` `the person`
    AND `it is a notifiable incident`
    AND `it arose out of the conduct of that business`

@export What is the most a business can be fined for not notifying the regulator of a notifiable incident (s 38(1))?
GIVEN `the business is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the business is a company or other body corporate
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most it can be fined for not notifying` IS
    IF `the business is a body corporate` THEN 55000 ELSE 12500

`hours to give a written notice once required` MEANS 48

`years the record of the incident must be kept` MEANS 5

@export Until what date must the business keep its record of the notifiable incident (s 38(7))?
GIVEN `the day notice was given to the regulator` IS A DATE @desc The day the business notified the regulator
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Until when must the record be kept` IS
    Date (DATE_DAY `the day notice was given to the regulator`)
         (DATE_MONTH `the day notice was given to the regulator`)
         (DATE_YEAR `the day notice was given to the regulator` PLUS `years the record of the incident must be kept`)

-- s 39(1): the person with management or control of the workplace "must ensure, so far as is reasonably practicable,
-- that the site where the incident occurred is not disturbed until an inspector arrives at the site or any earlier time
-- that an inspector directs." s 39(3): this "does not prevent any action -- (a) to assist an injured person; or (b) to
-- remove a deceased person; or (c) that is essential to make the site safe or to minimise the risk of a further
-- notifiable incident; or (d) that is associated with a police investigation; or (e) for which an inspector or the
-- regulator has given permission."
DECLARE `Action at the site` IS ONE OF
    `assisting an injured person`
    `removing a deceased person`
    `making the site safe or minimising the risk of a further incident`
    `a police investigation`
    `an action an inspector or the regulator has permitted`
    `any other action`

@export May the site of the incident be disturbed by this action (s 39)?
GIVEN `an inspector has arrived, or the time an inspector directed has passed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an inspector has arrived at the site, or any earlier time an inspector directed has come
      `the action` IS AN `Action at the site` @desc What is to be done at the site (mopping the stair, moving the officer, clearing the area)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the site be disturbed` IF
        `an inspector has arrived, or the time an inspector directed has passed`
    OR  (NOT (`the action` EQUALS `any other action`))

§§ `s 47 -- must I be consulted`

-- s 47(1): "The person conducting a business or undertaking must, so far as is reasonably practicable, consult ... with
-- workers who carry out work for the business or undertaking who are, or are likely to be, directly affected by a
-- matter relating to work health or safety." Penalty: individual $25 000; body corporate $115 000.
-- Section 49 lists when consultation is required (identifying hazards, deciding control measures, facilities, changes,
-- procedures ...): one input here.
@export Must the business consult me about this health or safety matter (s 47(1))?
GIVEN `I carry out work for the business` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you do work for the business
      `I am, or am likely to be, directly affected by the matter` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the work health or safety matter affects you directly, or is likely to
      `consulting me is reasonably practicable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is reasonably practicable to consult you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must the business consult me` IF
        `I carry out work for the business`
    AND `I am, or am likely to be, directly affected by the matter`
    AND `consulting me is reasonably practicable`

§§ `ss 84-88 -- my right to stop unsafe work`

-- s 84(1): "A worker may cease, or refuse to carry out, work if the worker has a reasonable concern that to carry out the
-- work would expose -- (a) the worker to a serious risk to their health or safety emanating from an immediate or
-- imminent exposure to a hazard; or (b) any other person to [the same]." s 84(2): a police officer must not if it
-- "would affect adversely, or could reasonably be expected to affect adversely, a covert operation or a dangerous
-- operation".
@export May I cease, or refuse to carry out, this work (s 84)?
GIVEN `I am a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker (s 7)
      `I reasonably fear a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard, to me or someone else` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have a reasonable concern that doing the work would expose you, or another person, to a serious risk to health or safety from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard
      `I am a police officer and stopping would or could harm a covert or dangerous operation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for police officers
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I cease or refuse this work` IF
        `I am a worker`
    AND `I reasonably fear a serious risk from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard, to me or someone else`
    AND (NOT `I am a police officer and stopping would or could harm a covert or dangerous operation`)

-- s 85(1): a health and safety representative "may direct a worker who is in a work group represented by the
-- representative to cease work if the representative has a reasonable concern that to carry out the work would expose
-- the worker to a serious risk ... from an immediate or imminent exposure to a hazard." (2): not "unless the matter is
-- not resolved after -- (a) consulting ... with the person conducting the business ...; and (b) attempting to resolve
-- the matter as an issue". (3): unless "the risk is so serious and immediate or imminent that it is not reasonable to
-- consult". (6): only if the representative has completed the prescribed initial training (or equivalent). (7): not if
-- compliance would adversely affect a covert or dangerous operation.
@export May my health and safety representative direct me to stop work (s 85)?
GIVEN `I am in the representative's work group` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative represents your work group
      `the representative reasonably fears a serious risk to me from an immediate or imminent hazard` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative has that reasonable concern about you
      `the representative consulted the business and tried to resolve the issue, without success` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative first consulted the business and tried to resolve the matter as an issue
      `the risk is too serious and immediate to consult first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the risk is so serious and immediate or imminent that consulting first is not reasonable
      `the representative has completed the initial training` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the representative has completed the prescribed initial training, or equivalent
      `the direction would or could harm a covert or dangerous operation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for police work
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May my representative direct me to stop work` IF
        `I am in the representative's work group`
    AND `the representative reasonably fears a serious risk to me from an immediate or imminent hazard`
    AND (`the representative consulted the business and tried to resolve the issue, without success` OR `the risk is too serious and immediate to consult first`)
    AND `the representative has completed the initial training`
    AND (NOT `the direction would or could harm a covert or dangerous operation`)

-- s 86: "A worker who ceases work under this Division must -- (a) as soon as practicable, notify the person conducting
-- the business or undertaking that the worker has ceased work under this Division unless the worker ceased work under a
-- direction from a health and safety representative; and (b) remain available to carry out suitable alternative work."
@export Must I tell the business, as soon as practicable, that I have stopped work (s 86(a))?
GIVEN `I stopped work under section 84 or 85` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you stopped or refused work under your own right (s 84) or on a representative's direction (s 85)
      `I stopped on a representative's direction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you stopped because your health and safety representative directed it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I tell the business I have stopped` IF
        `I stopped work under section 84 or 85`
    AND (NOT `I stopped on a representative's direction`)

-- s 88: "If a worker ceases work under this Division, that action does not affect the continuity of engagement of the
-- worker for prescribed purposes if the worker has not unreasonably failed to comply with a direction to carry out
-- suitable alternative work -- (a) at the same or another workplace; and (b) that was safe and appropriate for the
-- worker to carry out." The Act says what protects continuity; it does not say that anything else breaks it.
@export Is the continuity of my engagement protected after I stopped work (s 88)?
GIVEN `I stopped work under section 84 or 85` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you stopped or refused work under s 84 or s 85
      `I unreasonably failed to do suitable, safe and appropriate alternative work I was directed to do` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you unreasonably failed to follow a direction to do suitable alternative work that was safe and appropriate for you
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my continuity of engagement protected` IF
        `I stopped work under section 84 or 85`
    AND (NOT `I unreasonably failed to do suitable, safe and appropriate alternative work I was directed to do`)

§§ `ss 104-115 -- protection from discrimination for raising a safety issue`

-- s 105(1): a person engages in discriminatory conduct if it "(a) ... (i) dismisses a worker; or (ii) terminates a
-- contract for services with a worker; or (iii) puts a worker to the worker's detriment in the engagement of the worker;
-- or (iv) alters the position of a worker to the worker's detriment; or (b) ... (i) refuses or fails to offer to engage
-- a prospective worker; or (ii) treats a prospective worker less favourably ...; or (c) ... terminates a commercial
-- arrangement with another person; or (d) ... refuses or fails to enter into a commercial arrangement". s 105(2): also
-- if it "organises to take any action ... or threatens to organise or take that action".
DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `I was dismissed`
    `my contract for services was terminated`
    `I was put to my detriment in my engagement`
    `my position was altered to my detriment`
    `I was not offered engagement`
    `I was offered less favourable terms than another prospective worker`
    `a commercial arrangement with me was terminated`
    `a commercial arrangement with me was refused`
    `one of these was threatened or organised`
    `none of these`

-- s 106: conduct is for a prohibited reason "if it is engaged in because the worker ... (a) is, has been or proposes to
-- be a health and safety representative or a member of a health and safety committee; or (b) undertakes ... another
-- role under this Act; or (c), (d), (e), (f) exercises or performs, or refrains from exercising or performing, a power
-- or function under this Act ...; or (g) assists ... or gives ... information to, any person exercising a power or
-- performing a function under this Act; or (h) raises or has raised or proposes to raise an issue or concern about work
-- health and safety with -- (i) the person conducting a business or undertaking; or (ii) an inspector; ... (vi) another
-- worker; or (vii) any other person who has a duty under this Act in relation to the matter; ...; or (i) is involved
-- in ... resolving a work health and safety issue under this Act; or (j) is taking action ... to seek compliance by any
-- person with any duty or obligation under this Act."
DECLARE `Why it was done` IS ONE OF
    `I am, was or proposed to be a health and safety representative or committee member`
    `I took on another role under the Act`
    `I exercised, performed or refrained from a power or function under the Act`
    `I assisted or informed a person exercising a power under the Act`
    `I raised or proposed to raise a work health or safety issue or concern`
    `I was involved in resolving a work health and safety issue`
    `I took action to seek compliance with a duty under the Act`
    `for none of the prohibited reasons`

@export Was what was done to me discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason (ss 105, 106)?
GIVEN `what` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What the business or person did, or threatened, to you
      `why` IS A `Why it was done` @desc The reason it was done
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was it discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` IF
        (NOT (`what` EQUALS `none of these`))
    AND (NOT (`why` EQUALS `for none of the prohibited reasons`))

-- s 104(1): "A person must not engage in discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason." Penalty "(a) for an individual,
-- a fine of $115 000; (b) for a body corporate, a fine of $570 000." s 104(2): an offence "only if the reason ... was the
-- dominant reason". s 110(2): "The reason alleged for the discriminatory conduct is presumed to be the dominant reason
-- for that conduct unless the accused proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the reason was not the dominant
-- reason". Whether the accused proves that is for the court: an input.
@export Is what was done to me an offence under section 104 (ss 104, 110)?
GIVEN `what` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you
      `why` IS A `Why it was done` @desc The reason alleged
      `the accused proves the reason was not the dominant reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person accused proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the reason was not the dominant one
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is it a discrimination offence` IF
        `Was it discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` `what` `why`
    AND (NOT `the accused proves the reason was not the dominant reason`)

@export What is the most the person who discriminated against me, or coerced me, can be fined (ss 104, 107, 108)?
GIVEN `the person is a body corporate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is a company or other body corporate
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the most they can be fined for discrimination` IS
    IF `the person is a body corporate` THEN 570000 ELSE 115000

-- s 112(1)-(3): an eligible person may apply to the Tribunal, which may order compensation, reinstatement (for an
-- employee or prospective employee) or any other appropriate order. s 112(4): only "if a reason referred to in section
-- 106 was a substantial reason". s 113(1): "must be commenced not more than 1 year after the date on which the applicant
-- knew or ought to have known that the cause of action accrued." s 113(2): the prohibited reason "is presumed to be a
-- substantial reason ... unless the defendant proves" otherwise. s 113(3): a defence if the defendant proves "(a) the
-- conduct was reasonable in the circumstances; and (b) a substantial reason for the conduct was to comply with the
-- requirements of this Act". s 115: not if (a) a proceeding on the same matter under another law is on foot, (b)
-- compensation has been received for the matter under another law, or (c) a proceeding on the same matter under another
-- law has failed, "other than a proceeding ... relating to workers' compensation".
GIVEN `from` IS A DATE
      `to` IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`completed months from` MEANS
    (DATE_YEAR `to` MINUS DATE_YEAR `from`) TIMES 12
    PLUS (DATE_MONTH `to` MINUS DATE_MONTH `from`)
    MINUS (IF DATE_DAY `to` LESS THAN DATE_DAY `from` THEN 1 ELSE 0)

@export Is my application to the Tribunal within 1 year after I knew, or ought to have known, I had a cause of action (s 113(1))?
GIVEN `the day I knew or ought to have known` IS A DATE @desc The day you knew, or ought to have known, that the cause of action accrued
      `the day I apply` IS A DATE @desc The day you commence the proceeding
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is my Tribunal application in time` IF
        `completed months from` `the day I knew or ought to have known` `the day I apply` LESS THAN 12
    OR  (    `completed months from` `the day I knew or ought to have known` `the day I apply` EQUALS 12
         AND DATE_DAY `the day I apply` EQUALS DATE_DAY `the day I knew or ought to have known`)

DECLARE `Another proceeding` IS ONE OF
    `no other proceeding on the same matter`
    `another proceeding on the same matter is on foot`
    `I have received compensation for the matter under another law`
    `another proceeding on the same matter failed`
    `a workers' compensation proceeding on the same matter failed`

@export Can the Tribunal make an order for me for discriminatory conduct (ss 112, 113, 115)?
GIVEN `what` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you
      `why` IS A `Why it was done` @desc The reason alleged
      `the defendant proves the reason was not a substantial reason` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person you complain of proves, on the balance of probabilities, that the reason was not a substantial one
      `the defendant proves the conduct was reasonable and substantially to comply with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person proves the s 113(3) defence
      `my application is in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you apply within 1 year (s 113(1))
      `other proceedings` IS AN `Another proceeding` @desc Whether you have brought, won compensation in, or lost, another proceeding on the same matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the Tribunal make an order for me` IF
        `Was it discriminatory conduct for a prohibited reason` `what` `why`
    AND (NOT `the defendant proves the reason was not a substantial reason`)
    AND (NOT `the defendant proves the conduct was reasonable and substantially to comply with the Act`)
    AND `my application is in time`
    AND (    `other proceedings` EQUALS `no other proceeding on the same matter`
         OR  `other proceedings` EQUALS `a workers' compensation proceeding on the same matter failed`)

-- s 112(3)(b): the reinstatement order is available "in relation to a worker who was or is an employee or prospective
-- employee"; compensation and any other appropriate order are open to every eligible person.
@export Can the Tribunal order that I be reinstated or employed (s 112(3)(b))?
GIVEN `the Tribunal can make an order for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal can make an order for you (ss 112, 113, 115)
      `what` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done to you
      `I was or am an employee or prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were or are an employee, or applied to be one
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Can the Tribunal order my reinstatement` IF
        `the Tribunal can make an order for me`
    AND `I was or am an employee or prospective employee`
    AND (NOT (`what` EQUALS `a commercial arrangement with me was terminated`))
    AND (NOT (`what` EQUALS `a commercial arrangement with me was refused`))

§§ `ss 267, 272, 273 -- general rules that reach me`

-- s 267: "Except as provided in Part 6, nothing in this Act is to be construed as -- (a) conferring a right of action in
-- civil proceedings in relation to a contravention of a provision of this Act; or (b) conferring a defence to an action
-- in civil proceedings or otherwise affecting a right of action in civil proceedings". Part 6 is the discrimination
-- Part (ss 104-115). So a breach of s 19 or s 20 gives me no action under this Act; whether I may sue in negligence is
-- a matter for the general law and the Workers Compensation and Injury Management Act 2023, which this Act leaves as it
-- finds it.
@export Does this Act itself give me a right to sue for this contravention (s 267)?
GIVEN `the contravention is of Part 6 (discriminatory or coercive conduct)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether what you complain of is discriminatory, coercive or misleading conduct under Part 6
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the Act give me a right to sue` IF `the contravention is of Part 6 (discriminatory or coercive conduct)`

-- s 272: "A term of any agreement or contract that purports to exclude, limit or modify the operation of this Act or any
-- duty owed under this Act or to transfer to another person any duty owed under this Act is of no effect."
@export Does this term of my contract have effect (s 272)?
GIVEN `the term excludes, limits or modifies the Act or a duty under it, or transfers a duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the term tries to exclude, limit or change the Act or a duty owed under it, or to pass a duty to someone else
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does this term have effect` IF
    NOT `the term excludes, limits or modifies the Act or a duty under it, or transfers a duty`

-- s 273: "A person conducting a business or undertaking must not impose a levy or charge on a worker, or permit a levy or
-- charge to be imposed on a worker, for anything done, or provided, in relation to work health and safety." Penalty
-- "(a) for an individual, a fine of $5 500; (b) for a body corporate, a fine of $30 000."
@export May the business charge me for this (s 273)?
GIVEN `I am a worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a worker of the business
      `the charge is for something done or provided in relation to work health and safety` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the levy or charge is for safety equipment, training or anything else done or provided for work health and safety
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May the business charge me for this` IF
    NOT (`I am a worker` AND `the charge is for something done or provided in relation to work health and safety`)
